    },
    {
      "id": "EAIfiI8ZvyArM_KcGzPZd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 264.97571545510084,
      "y": 1759.4888159885102,
      "width": 1574.7976590870953,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "az",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 899044881,
      "version": 173,
      "versionNonce": 1216897009,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792838205919,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "7. Bottlenecks and Failures\n\n    1. Thundering Herd on Reconnection: If a major network outage resolves, millions of devices will simultaneously push their offline queues, overwhelming the\nAPI. Mitigation: The Client SDK must implement exponential backoff with full jitter before initiating the sync request.\n    \n    2. Device Clock Skew (Conflict Failures): Relying purely on client timestamps for conflict resolution is dangerous because user device clocks are frequently\ninaccurate. Mitigation: Implement Vector Clocks tracking versions per device (e.g., {\"deviceA\": 5, \"deviceB\": 2}), ensuring strict causality is respected during\nmerges.\n    \n    3. Connection Manager Scaling: Maintaining 300 million TCP connections requires massive memory. Mitigation: Terminate SSL at the Load Balancer layer to\nsave memory. Route WebSocket connections using Consistent Hashing on user_id so Kafka consumers instantly know which Connection Manager node holds a\nspecific user's sockets.\n    \n    4. Payload Bloat: Over time, fetching the entire config on every sync wastes bandwidth on mobile networks. Mitigation: The Sync Service tracks mutation\nhistory for the last N versions, allowing it to send only the specific JSON field deltas (server_mutations) rather than the entire 10 KB object.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "7. Bottlenecks and Failures\n\n    1. Thundering Herd on Reconnection: If a major network outage resolves, millions of devices will simultaneously push their offline queues, overwhelming the API. Mitigation: The Client SDK must implement exponential backoff with full jitter before initiating the sync request.\n    \n    2. Device Clock Skew (Conflict Failures): Relying purely on client timestamps for conflict resolution is dangerous because user device clocks are frequently inaccurate. Mitigation: Implement Vector Clocks tracking versions per device (e.g., {\"deviceA\": 5, \"deviceB\": 2}), ensuring strict causality is respected during merges.\n    \n    3. Connection Manager Scaling: Maintaining 300 million TCP connections requires massive memory. Mitigation: Terminate SSL at the Load Balancer layer to save memory. Route WebSocket connections using Consistent Hashing on user_id so Kafka consumers instantly know which Connection Manager node holds a specific user's sockets.\n    \n    4. Payload Bloat: Over time, fetching the entire config on every sync wastes bandwidth on mobile networks. Mitigation: The Sync Service tracks mutation history for the last N versions, allowing it to send only the specific JSON field deltas (server_mutations) rather than the entire 10 KB object.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "YJBamJ0By4ic8qM2N86cZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 484.08914084647495,
      "y": 1057.1968968002727,
      "width": 221.96626954332166,
      "height": 17.621575780365802,
      "angle": 0,
